{"key":"crop_soy","updated":"2026-10-20","label":"Soybeans","title":"Soybeans: area by parish","subtitle":"Share of each parish, USDA Cropland Data Layer 2023","source":"USDA NASS Cropland Data Layer 2023","demo":false,"build":2,"categories":[{"key":"n0","label":"Under 0.04%","note":"of the parish","color":"#eef4e6"},{"key":"n1","label":"0.04%–0.49%","note":"of the parish","color":"#cfe3bd"},{"key":"n2","label":"0.49%–2.2%","note":"of the parish","color":"#9ccb88"},{"key":"n3","label":"2.2%–5.5%","note":"of the parish","color":"#56a05f"},{"key":"n4","label":"5.5% or more","note":"of the parish","color":"#1f6b43"},{"key":"none","label":"None mapped","note":"under 1 acre","color":"#e5e0d3"},{"key":"not_rated","label":"No data","note":"","color":"#bdbdbd"}],"rank":{"high":"Most area","low":"Least area","note":"Ranked by acres."},"how":"Each parish is coloured by the share of its mapped area that is soybeans in 2023. Colours rank the parishes in fifths (the cut points are in the legend). Click a parish for the area in acres, its rank, and how the area changed over the last five years.","limits":["The Cropland Data Layer is a satellite map made once a year by USDA. Its accuracy is highest for the large crops (rice, soybeans, corn, cotton, sugarcane) and lower for small ones. NASS publishes accuracy tables for each year.","Each parish is the total of 30 m pixels inside it for 2023. A pixel can be wrongly labelled, and double crops (for example wheat then soybeans) count for both crops.","These are the crops mapped in one year. They are not a list of what a given farm grows and do not show rotations.","The most recent year is the last one NASS has published, so it is usually the previous growing season.","This crop includes double-crop classes, such as winter wheat followed by soybeans, so an acre can appear on two crop layers."],"credits":[{"text":"Crop maps: USDA National Agricultural Statistics Service, Cropland Data Layer 2023, summarised by parish with the CropScape s